tantra3 iccha

-- iccha: intention, will, the directive force.
--
-- iccha (Sanskrit) = wish, will, intention.
-- in samkhya: one of the three fundamental forces alongside jnana (knowing)
-- and kriya (doing). iccha fires first — it is the direction-setter.
-- without iccha, jnana has no target. kriya has no purpose.
--
-- here: the question's intention extracted from the expanded proof graph.
-- vidhi-kaala (imperative tense) IS the iccha-marker in the graph.
-- [find, vidhi-kaala, solve-for] means: "this is what we seek."
--
-- iccha returns three things:
--   has-intent   — was there an explicit vidhi-kaala? (true = the question was asked)
--   solve-for    — which concept is sought? (the target of the derivation)
--   scope-entity — whose frame? (whose owned properties to look through)
--
-- has-intent is the crucial discriminant.
-- "mass is 5 velocity is 10" → has-intent = false, solve-for = "mass" (fallback)
-- "find kinetic energy given mass 5 velocity 10" → has-intent = true, solve-for = "kinetic-energy"
--
-- when has-intent is false:
--   the question is ambiguous. "mass is 5, velocity is 10" could mean
--   KE or momentum — both are valid derivations from these janya.
--   without vidhi-kaala, the system should NOT pick arbitrarily.
--   the correct response is "no match" (awaiting clarification).
--
-- scope-entity is the lens:
--   "find KE of ball-A" — look only through ball-A's owned properties.
--   without scope: look through all flat graph sankhya values.
--
-- iccha calls extract-solve-for.

takes graph

result = extract-solve-for graph

return result

done
